diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index 3830d127d..b9127f178 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -311,6 +311,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - Avoid settling on bonuses (especially food/metal/high-production/commerce) unless the immediate overall site value clearly justifies it. See [KI#25](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#25---seemingly-fixed-ais-settling-too-much-and-too-often-on-bonuses-especially-food-bonuses--and-metals-and-other-high-production-bonuses-or-also-commerce-bonuses-to-a-lesser-extent).
 - **Unowned bonus extra valuation in city-site valuation:** for example, if we already own Wheat, a site with 1 Maize is **much better for the whole empire** than a city site with 2 more Wheat, which would have **more yields locally for this city** (e.g., ~+3-4 extra food per plot), **but much less long-term empire growth** (e.g., +1 Health +1 Health with Granary for all cities) (which is perhaps better even for this local city). Or, stone, happiness, or other bonuses could provide other advantages, for example **trading, military, accelerating buildables, etc**. Trade imports do not count as owned because they are not permanent. Applied once per bonus type and only after the first city. See [KI#147](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#147---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-settlers-not-adding-extra-valuation-to-unowned-bonuses-in-city-site-scoring). (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6025+) Update: now do not count as unowned the ones plausibly claimed by one of our existing cities' future BFC (see below bullet and [KI#178](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#178---fixedimproved-city-site-bonus-valuation-used-broad-trade-style-ai_bonusval-instead-of-settlement-specific-long-term-value)).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6025+) **City-site bonus value now uses settlement-specific long-term value instead of broad trade value:** the old non-yield path reused `AI_bonusVal`, which is useful for immediate bonus trading but can be wrong for founding cities (for example health may be mostly useless early but very valuable late game, but short-term trading only the value of a bonus right now matters, the deal may be cancelled anytime or a bonus get obsolete later, but cities are founded generally early, and permanently, so `AI_bonusVal` seems unfit to be used for settler valuation). So AdvCiv-SAS now values explicit bonus health/happiness plus ordinary early building effects such as Granary/Harbor, while excluding late/expensive buildings through a tunable prerequisite-era gate (e.g., we'll likely benefit from Health from Harbor if city is Coastal and has connected Crab, but the game may end before we build a Supermarket for some bonus' effect so don't value it). In save file 450 BBAI testing, `AI_bonusVal` was massively, seemingly incorrectly valuing Elephants over Crab, despite the Crab site being better locally (food from bonus and greener, vs a desert/plains/hill site to get Elephants), so the AI preferred `(18,40)` Elephants/Rice/Camel site instead of seemingly better stronger `(17,40)` with Crab/Rice/Camel, this change now helped Shaka successfully found uMgungundlovu at stronger `(17,40)`, the log showed Crab and Rice receiving explicit `happyHealth=300 buildingHappyHealth=150` instead of relying on one opaque `AI_bonusVal` spike. See [KI#178](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#178---fixedimproved-city-site-bonus-valuation-used-broad-trade-style-ai_bonusval-instead-of-settlement-specific-long-term-value).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6044+) Note: Special-building effects are estimated conservatively: religion-linked variants receive a lower per-point value because we are unlikely to build every overlapping variant, nonreligious special buildings receive more, and a shared cap prevents mod-added sets from inflating a site without bound; for example, Incense's 7 Cathedral happiness effects contribute 175 in total instead of 7 * 150 = 1050
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6025+) **Duplicate bonus value no longer over-counts reliable future-BFC coverage:** trade imports still do not count as owned as in Base AdvCiv, but bonuses already owned or plausibly claimed by one of our existing cities' future BFC no longer receive another full empire-wide duplicate value. Contested bonuses can still count when a known rival future BFC also threatens them, because ownership is not reliable. This prevents new cities from chasing a safe duplicate Crab (better go for bonuses we don't have to grow more) while still letting a contested Camel near Arabia matter (don't count on it empire-wide, but still locally there's a chance we might steal it so value it enough for local valuation only, not empire-wide one as not reliable enough for that). See [KI#178](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#178---fixedimproved-city-site-bonus-valuation-used-broad-trade-style-ai_bonusval-instead-of-settlement-specific-long-term-value).
 - **Settler site eval - devalue BFCs with many very bad tiles:** Candidate sites are **significantly devalued** (not auto-rejected) when enough non-home BFC plots are impassable or remain very low-yield after their best XML-valid improvement: the threshold as of now is dynamic `4 * food + 2 * production + commerce` (e.g., 1 food is not very bad, 1 hammer and 1 commerce is very bad, peak is very bad) (visible bonuses on usable plots are not counted because bonuses are likely to be always valuable). See [KI#26.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#262---enhanced-ai-settlers-not-devaluing-very-bad-non-home-tiles-peak-ice-cap-non-hill-desert-without-a-bonus-non-hill-snow-without-a-bonus-if-we-have-enough-of-them-in-bfc).
 - **First-city Good enough plots threshold**: AI settlers now also require the first-city site to have **enough** (e.g., >=13) **“good enough”** (i.e. tile is not impassable, not persistently barren by yield-potential logic, or is a reachable/already-improved water bonus) **BFC tiles** before accepting it, so the first-city is less likely to be founded on a long-term disadvantageous location.

